          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Are there eating habits that tend to make reflux worse?",
          "answer": "Based on the reference material I could search, several eating patterns are linked to reflux getting worse.\n\n**Large or late meals.** The general clinical reference on reflux (condition-gerd) lists large or late meals as a contributor. Symptoms often come after meals, and they are often worse when lying down. (The reference also notes that the pattern of symptoms, especially their link to meals and position, tells a clinician more than severity does.)\n\n**Common trigger foods.** The diet and meal-timing guidance (lifestyle-gerd-diet) lists coffee, chocolate, alcohol, citrus, tomato, mint, fatty or fried food, spicy food and carbonated drinks as foods often named as triggers. It also says the evidence that cutting out all of them helps is weak, and that a very restricted diet is hard to keep up. The approach it describes instead is working out which foods affect you personally.\n\n**Body weight.** The same diet guidance says weight reduction, where weight is above the healthy range, has the strongest supporting evidence of any lifestyle measure it covers.\n\nThe material I found did not cover how to identify your own triggers step by step, or how much meal size or timing changes things for any one person. A doctor or dietitian can help with that.\n\nIf you have heartburn or regurgitation that keeps coming back, or you have trouble swallowing, weight loss you did not expect, vomiting blood, or black stools, speak to a clinician promptly. Seek urgent care right away if you have chest pain, pain spreading to your arm or jaw, or shortness of breath, since these can also have causes other than reflux.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
